ithin the type `TraceCtx`\n    --> crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:89:19\n     |\n  89 | pub(crate) struct TraceCtx {\n     |                   ^^^^^^^^\nnote: required because it appears within the type `PhantomData<TraceCtx>`\n    --> /rustc/1159e78c4747b02ef996e55082b704c09b970588/library/core/src/marker.rs:823:12\nnote: required because it appears within the type `rules::alloc::raw_vec::RawVec<TraceCtx>`\n    --> /rustc/1159e78c4747b02ef996e55082b704c09b970588/library/alloc/src/raw_vec/mod.rs:74:19\nnote: required because it appears within the type `Vec<TraceCtx>`\n    --> /rustc/1159e78c4747b02ef996e55082b704c09b970588/library/alloc/src/vec/mod.rs:414:12\n     = note: required for `&Vec<TraceCtx>` to implement `std::marker::Send`\nnote: required because it's used within this closure\n    --> crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1907:44\n     |\n1907 |                 let logup_handle = s.spawn(|| -> Result<(), LogupZerocheckError> {\n     |                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nnote: required by a bound in `Scope::<'scope, 'env>::spawn`\n    --> /rustc/1159e78c4747b02ef996e55082b704c09b970588/library/std/src/thread/scoped.rs:195:5\n\nFor more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0277`.\nwarning: `openvm-cuda-backend` (lib) generated 1 warning\nerror: could not compile `openvm-cuda-backend` (lib) due to 2 previous errors; 1 warning emitted","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VdzZdsK1RpiETnFU9jEqAb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue is that the closure in `logup_handle` captures a reference to `early_eval_send` (which is `SendTraceVec`), and inside the closure it accesses `early_eval_send.0` which is `Vec<TraceCtx>`. The wrapper has `Sync`, but the issue is that the closure takes `&early_eval_send` and then accesses the inner `Vec<TraceCtx>`. The `SendTraceVec` is `Sync`, but the compiler is looking through the wrapper to the inner `Vec<TraceCtx>` which is not `Sync` because `TraceCtx` contains raw pointers.\n\n\nActually, I need to reconsider this. The closure captures `late_logup_send` and `early_eval_send` by reference, and even though `SendTraceVec` implements `Sync`, the compiler is still checking whether the captured references themselves are `Send` when the closure is moved across threads. The closure is capturing individual fields rather than the whole wrapper types, so Rust sees `Vec<&TraceCtx>` directly instead of trusting the `Sync` bound on `SendTraceVec`. I need to figure out whether the inner vector type itself needs to be `Sync`, or if there's a way to force the compiler to see the wrapper's trait bounds instead. Vec<&TraceCtx>` needs to be `Send`, which requires `Vec<&TraceCtx>: Sync`, which requires `&TraceCtx: Sync`, which requires `TraceCtx: Sync`, which fails because of raw pointers.\n\nSimilarly for `early_eval_send.0` - it captures the field `.0` directly, which is `Vec<TraceCtx>`, and `&Vec<TraceCtx>` needs `Send`, requiring `Vec<TraceCtx>: Sync`.\n\nThe simplest solution is to add `Send` and `Sync` impls directly to `TraceCtx`. The raw pointers point to GPU global memory that's accessible from any stream, which is the same justification used throughout the codebase for other structs with these impls.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_